{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793438436,"nonce":"oqXCkQKgALJCpjc+LVfRMM5Y8kY=","title":"Python chaos: the untimed image save loses the wall column","message":"chaos's iterations beat PyPy 7x (CI Linux 9f18acb1: 23.6 ms for five against 163), yet the whole process loses, 309.9 against 305.5 ms: zypy spends 286 ms outside the timed loop to PyPy's 142. With -n 0 (no iterations) the NUC gives zypy 0.39 to 0.42 s, PyPy 0.14 s.\n\nWhat runs outside the loop is `im = [[1] * h for i in range(w)]` (1000 lists of 1200) and save_im, which writes 1.2M pixels as `f.write(b'%c%c%c' % (c, c, c))`. Isolated on the NUC (two runs each, zypy / PyPy):\n- list-of-lists allocation 14 to 20 ms / 5 to 6 ms\n- save_im 195 to 210 ms / 83 ms\n- 1.2M `b'%c%c%c' % (i \u0026 255, 7, 9)` alone 119 to 124 ms / 20 to 29 ms\n- 1.2M `f.write(b'abc')` alone 47 to 48 ms / 50 to 52 ms\n\nSo the bytes `%` formatting is about 100 ns a call against PyPy's 20: the format string is a literal, so the conversions and the result length are known at compile time and the tuple need not be built. The file writes are at parity.","files":null}]}